27.1  Where environment variables come from

TL;DR
  - Every process gets a copy of its parent's environment: a list of NAME=value
    strings.
  - export NAME=value sets it for the current shell and the programs it starts,
    not for its parent or for other terminals.
  - To keep it, put the export in a startup file (27.2). For graphical apps on
    systemd desktops, use a file in ~/.config/environment.d/.

      printenv LANG
      export EDITOR=nvim
      LANG=C sort names.txt    # set for one command only

On your machine
  SHELL     /bin/bash
  HOME      /home/alice
  LANG      en_US.UTF-8
  EDITOR    nvim
  TERM      xterm-256color
  Login     /bin/zsh (SHELL says /bin/bash: changed since login?)
  In all    8 variables set; only well-known ones are shown here

Pitfalls
  - A variable set without export stays in the shell: the programs it starts
    don't see it.
  - A change never reaches programs already running, nor other terminals: open a
    new one, or source ~/.bashrc.
  - systemd services don't read your shell files: give them variables with
    Environment= in a drop-in (see 22.3).
  - sudo resets most of the environment. sudo -E keeps it, when sudoers allows
    it.
  - Variables are visible to every process you run, in /proc/PID/environ: avoid
    keeping secrets there for long.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 27.1 --why

Related
  Commands  env, printenv, export
  Paths     /etc/environment, ~/.config/environment.d/
  See also  idk 27.2 Shell startup files
  See also  idk 23.3 PATH and command resolution
